`this.commandLine.fileNames.includes(fileName)` is O(n) per file. For a project with thousands of source files this is called in a loop over `fileNames.length` entries on every `extractPropsFromStories` call. Use a `Set<string>` alongside `commandLine.fileNames` to make the membership check O(1):
```ts
private fileNameSet = new Set<string>();
// in ensureFiles:
if (!this.fileNameSet.has(fileName)) {
  this.commandLine.fileNames.push(fileName);
  this.fileNameSet.add(fileName);
  added = true;
}
```

In `unwrapToFunctionAST`, the fallback for call expressions (after the `Object.assign` guard) takes `node.arguments[0]` for *any* call expression:
```ts
// forwardRef(...), memo(...) — first arg is the function
if (node.arguments.length >= 1) {
  return unwrapToFunctionAST(typescript, node.arguments[0], varMap, depth + 1);
}
```
For a HOC like `createComponent(Config, () => <div/>)`, this would attempt to unwrap `Config` (the first argument) rather than the render function. It will fail gracefully (returning `undefined`) for this specific case, but could silently produce wrong defaults if the first argument happens to be a function of a different shape. Listing known safe HOC names explicitly (`forwardRef`, `memo`, `lazy`) would be safer.
